::: {.doc-subject}

Find a minimal set of nodes that d-separates two others in a directed acyclic graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L67258-L67385){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.find_minimal_d_separator(
    x,
    y,
    included=None,
    restricted=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Find minimal d-separator](../assets/graphs/find-minimal-d-separator.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A causal graph: age (teal) affects exercise, exercise affects weight, and both exercise and weight affect heart health (violet). Every path from age to heart starts with the edge into exercise, so conditioning on exercise alone, in the accent color, blocks them all. The card returns the minimal separator [exercise]." width="100%"}

Returns a minimal d-separator of `x` and `y` (see `is_d_separator()` and
`is_minimal_d_separator()`): a set of nodes that, once conditioned on, blocks every path
between them, and from which no node can be dropped. In causal inference this is a lean
set of variables to measure or adjust for. Constraints mirror real studies: `included=`
forces variables into the set (for example, ones that are always recorded) and
`restricted=` limits the set to observable variables.

When `x` and `y` are adjacent, or every separator would need a forbidden node, no
separator exists and `None` is returned.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[x]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   Nodes, or lists of nodes, as IDs or labels.

<code>[y]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   Nodes, or lists of nodes, as IDs or labels.

<code>[included]{.doc-parameter-name}[=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op}[None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional nodes that must be in the separator.

<code>[restricted]{.doc-parameter-name}[=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op}[None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional nodes that the separator may use (default: all nodes).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[int] or None]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The node IDs of a minimal separator, sorted, or `None` if none exists.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is not a directed acyclic graph, a node does not exist, `x`, `y` and
    `included` overlap, or `included` is not part of `restricted`.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The linear-time algorithm of van der Zander, Liśkiewicz and Textor (2019), as in
NetworkX: start from all allowed ancestors of `x`, `y` and `included`, keep those
reachable from `x` by open paths, then those also reachable from `y`. Each step is a
graph search, so the whole computation runs in $O(V + E)$ time.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_d_separator`) 

:   Check a separator.

[](`~is_minimal_d_separator`) 

:   Check that a separator is minimal.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

In a chain with a shortcut through `D`, both `B` and `D` are needed:

::: {#a62bf9e5 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("A", "D"), ("D", "C")], directed=True
)
found = graph.find_minimal_d_separator("A", "C")

[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in found]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['B', 'D']
```
:::
:::


### What to adjust for

`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each
cause to its effect. To separate Exercise from Heart disease, Weight blocks the causal
path and Age the backdoor path through Blood pressure:

::: {#93a12055 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal = ec.causal()
found = causal.find_minimal_d_separator("Exercise", "Heart disease")

[causal.get_label_from_id(n) for n in found]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
['Age', 'Weight']
```
:::
:::


Blood pressure and Heart disease share the cause Genetics, which can't be measured. To
estimate the effect of one on the other, drop the arrow between them and look for a set
that separates them in what is left, blocking every backdoor path. Genetics is the only
one, so with the separator restricted to the observed variables, none exists:

::: {#8bc5b010 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import polars as pl

backdoor = ec.causal().delete_edge("Blood pressure", "Heart disease")
observed = (
    backdoor.get_node_df().filter(pl.col("observed"))["id"].to_list()
)

pair = ("Blood pressure", "Heart disease")
found = backdoor.find_minimal_d_separator(*pair)

(
    [backdoor.get_label_from_id(n) for n in found],
    backdoor.find_minimal_d_separator(*pair, restricted=observed),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(['Genetics'], None)
```
:::
:::


::: {#690eb687 .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal.plot(
    layout="pos", color_by="observed",
    highlight=[
        ("Genetics", "Blood pressure"), ("Genetics", "Heart disease")
    ],
    label_by="label", height=360,
)
```

